<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #eaf2f8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I'm Excited! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> I'm Happy. <b>Theme:</b> I'm Excited!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td colspan="2" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: productive: Practice tracing and copying letters of the alphabet. interactive: Engage in interactive labeling activities.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can trace letters and words that describe emotions, with teacher guidance (e.g., 'sad' or 'happy'). Can engage in interactive labeling activities by matching words to pictures of emotions (e.g., labeling 'happy' under a smiling face). Can draw and label facial expressions (e.g., 'sad' or 'angry').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Good morning, everyone!" and asks "How are you?" while showing a big 'happy' face. Students respond chorally "I'm happy!" and perform a happy gesture (e.g., big smile, thumbs up). The teacher then quickly introduces the feelings song ("How Do You Feel Today?") with TPR actions for 'happy,' 'sad,' 'tired,' and 'excited.' Students sing along and imitate actions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a large flashcard with the word 'happy' written clearly, next to a picture of a smiling face. The teacher explicitly models how to trace the letters in 'happy' on a large whiteboard or laminated sheet, saying each letter sound (H-A-P-P-Y) and the whole word. The teacher also models the pronunciation /h/ in 'happy' and /e/ in 'excited' clearly. Then, the teacher shows a worksheet with dotted lines for tracing the word 'happy' and demonstrates how to hold a pencil and trace the word slowly and carefully.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the word 'happy' on the flashcard) "Is this the word 'sad'?" (Expected answer: "No! Happy!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the dotted lines for 'happy' on the worksheet) "Do we trace or draw our own lines?" (Expected answer: "Trace!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5-7 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "I'm Happy."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large picture cards showing different facial expressions (happy, sad, excited, angry) with the corresponding word written below each picture. A simple chart or poster titled "How Do You Feel?" displaying these words and images.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher displays the picture cards one by one, clearly stating the emotion and the word: "This is a happy face, and this word says 'happy'." "This is an excited face, and this word says 'excited'." The teacher emphasizes recognizing the initial letters (e.g., 'H' in 'happy', 'E' in 'excited').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing feelings and needs by saying, 'I'm happy.' or 'I'm excited.' Responding to questions about feelings: Answering 'How are you?' with simple 'I'm...' statements.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher calls out an emotion (e.g., "Show me happy!"). Students point to the correct picture card and the written word.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "Which word says 'sad'?" and students point to the word 'sad' on the chart. The teacher can also ask, "How are you?" while showing a card, and students respond, "I'm happy!" or "I'm excited!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher guides students to recognize the first letter of familiar words, like 'H' in 'happy' and 'E' in 'excited,' pointing them out on the cards.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Tracing and copying words that describe emotions ('happy', 'sad', 'excited', 'angry'). Simple sentences like 'I'm happy.' Phonemic awareness: Recognizing letters of the alphabet in familiar words (e.g., 'E' in the word 'rest' or 'H' in the word 'happy').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Tracing):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with large, dotted letters for 'happy' and 'sad.' Each word is paired with a corresponding facial expression drawing. Students are instructed to trace each letter carefully with their pencils. The teacher circulates, offering individual help and encouraging students to say the word as they trace it. The teacher reinforces the pronunciation of /h/ and /e/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled Practice - Tracing and Copying):</b> Students receive a new worksheet. This worksheet has a picture of an 'excited' face and an 'angry' face. Below each face, there's a dotted line to trace the word (e.g., 'excited', 'angry'), and then an empty line to copy the word. The teacher guides students through tracing, then encourages them to copy the word independently in the space provided. The teacher points out the letter 'E' in 'excited' and asks students to find it.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Interactive Labeling & Peer Check):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with three empty boxes and three small pictures of different emotions (happy, sad, excited) at the bottom. Students cut out the pictures. The teacher writes the words 'happy', 'sad', 'excited' on the board. Students paste each picture into a box, then either trace the word written below the box (if provided with dotted words) or attempt to copy the word from the board next to their picture. Students then turn to a friend (peer check) and point to their work, saying, "This is happy!" or "I'm sad." while showing their drawing and word.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[15 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "I'm Happy."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual drawing and labeling activity using simple sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher distributes blank paper (or paper with a pre-drawn circle for a face). Students are asked to "Draw how you feel today!" (connecting to the Theme "I'm Excited!" and Scenario "I'm Happy."). They draw a facial expression showing an emotion they have learned (e.g., happy, sad, excited, angry). After drawing, the teacher guides them to choose one of the practiced emotion words from the board (e.g., 'happy', 'sad', 'excited') and to either trace the word (if provided as a stencil or dotted) or copy it below their drawing. For advanced learners, they can attempt to write "I'm happy" or "I'm excited." The teacher encourages them to share their drawing with a partner and say, "I'm [emotion]!" or "This is a [emotion] face." This allows them to apply their writing skills to express a feeling, integrating the pragmatic function of expressing emotions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[8-10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during tracing, copying, and labeling activities. Collection of student drawings/worksheets for review.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete a "My Feelings" worksheet. It has three blank boxes. For each box, students draw a facial expression (e.g., happy, sad, angry) and then trace or copy the corresponding emotion word below it. They are expected to use at least two different emotion words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can trace letters and short words legibly (e.g., 'happy', 'sad').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can copy familiar emotion words from a model.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can match a drawn facial expression to the correct written emotion word.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to label drawings with emotion words.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students hold up a "Thumbs Up" if they feel they can trace and copy the word 'happy' or 'sad.' They hold up a "Thumbs Down" if they need more practice. The teacher can ask: "What word did you write today?" and students show their paper or say the word.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[5 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Consolidation, personal meaning-making, and connection to future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite feeling word to write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show me a happy face and say 'I'm happy'?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in engaging students with writing tasks? The use of drawings and tracing lines seemed to be effective.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students still struggle with pencil grip and fine motor skills for tracing. More large-motor tracing activities (e.g., finger tracing in sand/air) might be beneficial. Need to reinforce letter recognition for all target words more consistently.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Mediation & Integration), students will use their learned vocabulary and writing skills to create a small "Feelings Book," consolidating their understanding of emotions and ability to write simple words/phrases.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[2-3 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>